which is typical in delay calculators).[17][21][23][24][25][27][28][29][30]\n\n### 114 BPM\n\n- Quarter: \\(60000 / 114 ≈ 526.3\\) ms  \n- Eighth: \\(526.3 × 0.5 ≈ 263.2\\) ms  \n- Dotted eighth: \\(526.3 × 0.75 ≈ 394.7\\) ms  \n- 3/16: same as dotted eighth ≈ **394.7 ms**  \n- Triplet eighth: \\(526.3 × 0.3333 ≈ 175.4\\) ms  \n- Sixteenth: \\(526.3 × 0.25 ≈ 131.6\\) ms  \n\n### 115 BPM\n\n- Quarter: \\(60000 / 115 ≈ 521.7\\) ms  \n- Eighth: ≈ **260.9 ms**  \n- Dotted eighth (3/16): ≈ **391.3 ms**  \n- Triplet eighth: ≈ **173.9 ms**  \n- Sixteenth: ≈ **130.4 ms**  \n\n### 116 BPM\n\n- Quarter: \\(60000 / 116 ≈ 517.2\\) ms  \n- Eighth: ≈ **258.6 ms**  \n- Dotted eighth (3/16): ≈ **387.9 ms**  \n- Triplet eighth: ≈ **172.4 ms**  \n- Sixteenth: ≈ **129.3 ms**  \n\n### 117 BPM\n\n- Quarter: \\(60000 / 117 ≈ 512.8\\) ms  \n- Eighth: ≈ **256.4 ms**  \n- Dotted eighth (3/16): ≈ **384.6 ms**  \n- Triplet eighth: ≈ **170.9 ms**  \n- Sixteenth: ≈ **128.2 ms**  \n\n### 118 BPM\n\n- Quarter: \\(60000 / 118 ≈ 508.5\\) ms  \n- Eighth: ≈ **254.2 ms**  \n- Dotted eighth (3/16): ≈ **381.4 ms**  \n- Triplet eighth: ≈ **169.5 ms**  \n- Sixteenth: ≈ **127.1 ms**  \n\nThese numbers follow the same quarter‑note base as the online tables (e.g. “quarter = 60000 ÷ BPM; dotted = ×1.5; triplet = ×⅔”).[19][23][24][25][27]\n\n\n---\n\n## Typical dub delay feedback percentages\n\nDedicated dub‑style delay tutorials and plugin docs emphasize very **high feedback**, close to self‑oscillation:\n\n- A Buhr Audio guide for *classic dub echo* recommends **feedback 60–90%**, “right up to the edge of self‑oscillation.”[5]  \n- A dub delay walkthrough (Finish More Music) sets feedback “somewhere around **70%**” and describes “on the dub delay again sort of **3/4 of the way round**,” i.e. roughly 75% for very long trails.[3]  \n- TAL‑Dub II / TAL‑Dub‑X manuals allow feedback **>1 (up to 2)** specifically to create *endless delays* or delays that *get louder with time*.[6][7]\n\nSo for **dub delays**, typical ranges are:\n\n- **General dub echo**: about **60–80% feedback** as a working starting point.[3][5]  \n- **More extreme/self‑oscillating**: **80–95%+**, or even feedback factors above 1.0 in plugins that support it (TAL‑Dub).[6][7]  \n\nIn practice, many producers keep feedback just below runaway self‑oscillation and use filters to keep the repeats under control.[3][5][6][7][8]\n\n\n---\n\n## Filtering in the feedback path (high‑pass and low‑pass cutoffs)\n\nDub delay tones usually come from **strong band‑limiting** in the feedback path, mimicking tape/analog echoes and preventing low‑end buildup and harsh highs.\n\nThe exact cutoffs vary by plugin, but several references show the typical design:\n\n- TAL‑Dub II manual:  \n  - Feedback runs through a **non‑linear 6 dB low‑pass filter with resonance** and a **3 dB high‑pass filter**.[6]  \n- TAL‑Dub‑X manual:  \n  - Filter section “contains a **high‑pass filter and a 6 dB resonance low‑pass**” in the delay path.[7]  \n- Spectrasonics *Radio Delay* shows a typical parameter range:  \n  - HPF cutoff 20–10,000 Hz  \n  - LPF cutoff 100–22,000 Hz[10]  \n  This reflects how delay plugins usually expose both HPF and LPF in the feedback path.\n\nFrom dub tutorials and common practice (as well as the above ranges), the **typical dub settings** are roughly:\n\n- **High‑pass filter (HPF)** in the feedback path:  \n  - Often around **150–300 Hz** to clear kick/bass and keep the echo from muddying the low end.  \n  - Some use higher (300–500 Hz) for very thin, “telephone” style dub delays, especially on chords or vocals.  \n- **Low‑pass filter (LPF)** in the feedback path:  \n  - Commonly **3–6 kHz** for dark, tape‑like repeats.  \n  - Some dub techno setups go even lower (2–4 kHz) to make the repeats very mellow and sit behind the drums.\n\nThese exact cutoff numbers are not always spelled out in text, but are consistent with delay plugin filter ranges and the described goal of “dark, filtered echoes.”[6][7][8][10][12]\n\n\n---\n\n## What “ping‑pong” delay means\n\n“Ping‑pong” refers to how the repeats move across the stereo field:\n\n- Mixgraph’s glossary: a ping‑pong delay “alternates its repeats between the left and right channels, so each echo **bounces across the stereo field** instead of stacking in the middle.”[1]  \n- Guitar Goblin: “digital, stereo delay effect that **bounces the delayed sound from left to right** with panning in stereo channels.”[2]  \n- KVR and Reddit discussions describe it as two delay lines panned left/right where each repeat is sent to the opposite side, creating a back‑and‑forth stereo echo.[4][9]  \n- A Cubase explanation video: “repeated echoes bounce back and forth between the left and right in the stereo field.”[11]  \n\nSo in practice:\n\n- **Ping‑pong delay** = a stereo delay where each successive repeat is routed to the opposite channel, **alternating left–right** and creating a wide, moving image rather than centered echoes.[1][2][4][9][11][14]\n\n\n---\n\n## Typical reverb pre‑delay and decay for dub techno chords\n\nDub techno chords usually sit in a **large, spacious but rhythmically aware reverb**, often with tempo‑related pre‑delay so the chord “hits” cleanly then blooms.\n\nGeneral reverb technique references give useful ranges:\n\n- A reverb techniques article lists typical **pre‑delay** values from **0–100 ms**, with guidelines:[15]  \n  - Short (10–30 ms): slight separation, natural  \n  - Medium (30–60 ms): clear separation, punchy  \n  - Long (60–100+ ms): dramatic, distinct slap‑back  \n  It specifically notes matching pre‑delay to tempo, e.g. at 120 BPM:  \n  - 1/64 ≈ 20 ms  \n  - 1/32 ≈ 40 ms  \n  - 1/16 ≈ 80 ms[15]  \n- For **decay (RT60)**, the same article gives broad ranges: **0.1–10+ s**, with “long hall/plate” reverb for atmosphere typically **3–5 s decay, 40–80 ms pre‑delay**.[15]\n\nDub‑techno tutorials and presets (e.g. Ableton‑based dub techno tutorials) usually describe:\n\n- **Long decay**: often **3–6 s** on a hall or plate to create the characteristic wash around the chords, in line with the “long hall/plate” category.[15]  \n- **Pre‑delay**: often **30–80 ms**, roughly matching a **1/32–1/16 note** at common dub tempos (110–125 BPM), so the dry chord transient is heard before the reverb cloud.[15][12]\n\nSynthesizing these references and common practice:\n\n- For **dub techno chords** at your BPM range (114–118):  \n  - **Pre‑delay**: about **30–80 ms**, often near a musical subdivision (≈40 ms ≈ 1/32; ≈80 ms ≈ 1/16 at ~120 BPM).[15]  \n  - **Decay time**: typically **3–5 s**, sometimes longer for very deep, Berlin‑style dub atmospheres.[15][12]\n\nThese values give a clear rhythmic definition to chords while still creating the very large, hazy space associated with dub techno.\n\n\n---\n\n## Web pages used (with URLs)\n\nBelow are the specific pages used to derive formulas, values, and concepts:\n\n**Delay time formulas and note divisions**\n\n- SuperGlobalCalculator – BPM to Delay Time Calculator (ms per beat = 60000 ÷ BPM)[17]  \n  URL: `https://superglobalcalculator.com/calculators/music/bpm-delay/`  \n- Sengpiel Audio – BPM tempo and delay to time conversion (quarter note = 60000 ÷ BPM)[20]  \n  URL: `https://sengpielaudio.com/calculator-bpmtempotime.htm`  \n- Caesar Cipher – Delay Time Calculator — BPM to Milliseconds (quarter note = 60000 ÷ BPM; other notes as multiples; dotted = ×1.5)[21]  \n  URL: `https://caesarcipher.org/calculators/delay-time-calculator`  \n- Mixgraph – BPM to Milliseconds Converter (one beat = 60000 ÷ BPM; other values by halving/doubling)[23]  \n  URL: `https://www.mixgraph.io/tools/bpm-to-ms`  \n- Tunelens – Delay Time Calculator (quarter note = 60000 ÷ BPM; half/double for other divisions).[24]  \n  URL: `https://tunelens.com/delay-time-calculator.html`  \n- Darwin’s Cat – Delay Time Calculator (quarter note = 60000 ÷ BPM; dotted = ×1.5; triplet = ×⅔; example dotted eighth at 120 BPM).[25]  \n  URL: `https://darwinscat.com/sound-utils/bpm-delay-calculator`  \n- Patchbay – BPM to Delay Time Chart (dotted = ×1.5, triplet = ×⅔).[27]  \n  URL: `https://clintjohnson.cloud/patchbay/reference/bpm-to-delay-time/`  \n- Uptrack – Delay Time Calculator (ms = 60000 ÷ BPM × note multiplier).[28]  \n  URL: `https://uptrack.pro/tools/delay-calculator`  \n- NickFever – Delay & Reverb Time Calculator (uses 60000 ÷ BPM).[29]  \n  URL: `https://nickfever.com/music/delay-calculator`  \n- Jukeblocks – Delay Time Calculator + Reverb Time Calculator (formula: delay time = 60000/BPM × note division).[30]  \n  URL: `https://jukeblocks.io/free-tools/delay-and-reverb-time-calculator/`  \n\n**Dub delay feedback, filters, and ping‑pong**\n\n- Buhr Audio – How to Get a Dub Delay (recommends feedback 60–90%).[5]  \n  URL: `https://buhraudio.com/blog/how-to-get-a-dub-delay`  \n- Finish More Music – Dub Delay walkthrough (sets feedback around 70%, “3/4 of the way round”).[3]  \n  URL: `https://finishmoremusic.com/dub-delay/`  \n- TAL‑Dub II User Manual (non‑linear 6 dB low‑pass with resonance and 3 dB high‑pass in delay path).[6]  \n  URL: `https://fr.scribd.com/document/458874884/TAL-Dub-II-User-Manual-EN`  \n- TAL‑Dub‑X User Manual (HPF + 6 dB resonance LPF in feedback path; feedback up to 2.0 for endless delays).[7]  \n  URL: `https://www.bhphotovideo.com/lit_files/768198.pdf`  \n- Spectrasonics Trilian – Radio Delay (HPF range 20–10000 Hz; LPF range 100–22000 Hz).[10]  \n  URL: `https://support.spectrasonics.net/manual/Trilian/1.5/en/topic/radio-delay`  \n- Spectrasonics Omnisphere – BPM Delay (feedback parameter; tone control selects HPF/LPF).[8]  \n  URL: `https://support.spectrasonics.net/manual/Omnisphere/fx_page/delay/page29.html`  \n- Mixgraph glossary – Ping‑pong delay definition (alternates repeats left/right).[1]  \n  URL: `https://www.mixgraph.io/glossary/ping-pong-delay`  \n- Guitar Goblin – Ping Pong Delay (bounces delayed sound left/right via stereo panning).[2]  \n  URL: `https://guitargoblin.com/ping-pong-delay/`  \n- KVR Audio forum – What is a Ping Pong Delay? (explains two‑delay, left/right alternating design).[4]  \n  URL: `https://www.kvraudio.com/forum/viewtopic.php?t=288792`  \n- Reddit /r/ableton – discussion of ping‑pong vs auto‑panning delays.[9]  \n  URL: `https://www.reddit.com/r/ableton/comments/96fkpk/what_is_the_difference_between_ping_pong_delay/`  \n- Cubase “This is Ping Pong Delay” video (bounces echoes left/right).[11]  \n  URL: `https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=1KzbITFAgP4&vl=en`  \n- “Ping Pong Delay Secrets” video (describes stereo bouncing).[14]  \n  URL: `https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fqd1frVCAj4`  \n- Dub Techno Tutorial (Ableton) – uses ping‑pong delay with low dry/wet, high feedback, and filtered band for dub chords.[12]  \n  URL: `https://www.studiobrootle.com/dub-techno-tutorial-ableton/`  \n\n**Reverb pre‑delay and decay**\n\n- Plugg‑Supply – Reverb Techniques and Tricks (gives pre‑delay 0–100 ms; decay ranges, and tempo‑matched pre‑delay examples: 1/64 ≈ 20 ms, 1/32 ≈ 40 ms, 1/16 ≈ 80 ms at 120 BPM; typical short/medium/long room and hall settings).[15]  \n  URL: `https://plugg-supply.net/articles/reverb-techniques-and-tricks`  \n\nIf you’d like, I can extend the table with additional note divisions (e.g. quarter‑note dotted, quarter‑note triplet, 1/32, etc.) at your BPM range, or translate these into exact settings for a specific plugin (Ableton Echo, Native Instruments Replika XT, etc.).","refusal":null,"reasoning":null,"annotations":[{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://www.mixgraph.io/glossary/ping-pong-delay","title":"What is a ping-pong delay? Definition and ...","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://guitargoblin.com/ping-pong-delay/","title":"Ping Pong Delay – What Is It And How Does It Work?","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_citation","url_citation":{"url":"https://finishmoremusic.com/dub-delay/","title":"Dub Delay","start_index":0,"end_index":0}},{"type":"url_cita